# expand — real-world examples

## Convert leading spaces to tabs in a file

```sh
# unexpand: spaces -> tabs; -a converts all runs, not just leading
unexpand -a myfile > mynewfile
```

## Convert tabs to spaces (the expand direction)

```sh
expand myfile > mynewfile

# Use 2-space tab stops
expand -t 2 myfile > mynewfile
```

## Only convert leading tabs, leave inline ones alone

```sh
expand -i myfile > mynewfile
```

## Homelab: normalize a Makefile-adjacent script that mixed tabs and spaces

```sh
expand -t 4 deploy.sh > deploy.fixed && mv deploy.fixed deploy.sh
```

## Homelab: fix YAML that fails to parse because of tabs

```sh
expand -t 2 docker-compose.yml > /tmp/fixed.yml && mv /tmp/fixed.yml docker-compose.yml
```

| Command / flag | What it does                    |
| -------------- | ------------------------------- |
| expand         | tabs → spaces                   |
| unexpand       | spaces → tabs                   |
| -t N           | tab stops every N columns       |
| -a (unexpand)  | convert all whitespace runs     |
| -i (expand)    | only leading tabs               |
